From: lichenggang <lichenggang@uniontech.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Nov 2026 20:20:00 +0800
Subject: Do not build with -march=native
Forwarded: not-needed

The Unix-like branch of the global compile options hardcodes both
-march=native and -mtune=native.  That is fine for a local build on the
machine that will run the code, but it is wrong for a binary distribution:
the flags make the compiler emit every instruction the build machine
supports, so a library built on an AVX-512 host contains AVX-512
instructions and dies with SIGILL on any older CPU of the same
architecture.  The flags also make the output depend on the builder, so
the package cannot be reproduced.

Drop both flags for the Unix-like path.  The Windows/MSVC branches keep
their explicit /arch: selection, which is a deliberate per-architecture
choice rather than a guess about the build host.

---
 CMakeLists.txt | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -198,10 +198,14 @@ if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "Windows")
     endif()
 else()
     # Set global compile options
+    # -march=native/-mtune=native are intentionally not used here: this path is
+    # taken by Unix-like distributions building binary packages, where the
+    # instructions emitted on the builder must also run on older CPUs of the
+    # same architecture, and the output has to be reproducible.
     add_compile_options(
         -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wmissing-prototypes -Wredundant-decls -Wshadow -fPIC
-        -Wpointer-arith -Wa,--noexecstack -fomit-frame-pointer -O3 -mtune=native
-        -march=native -finline-functions
+        -Wpointer-arith -Wa,--noexecstack -fomit-frame-pointer -O3
+        -finline-functions
     )
 endif()
 
